  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... While the probability for coastal cyclogenesis off \nthe South Atlantic coast this weekend is relatively high, forecast \nconfidence in impacts/rain over cntl NC remains only low to medium.  \n\nAside from the development and over-amplification of spurious \nfeatures (from gridscale/convective feedback) that have riddled the \nGFS model in particular with this weekend's probable coastal low, \nthere has not been any appreciable change in the background synoptic \npattern and features --and forecaster rationale-- contributing to \nthat cyclone development. \n\nThe models continue to indicate a shortwave perturbation will \ndevelop and amplify, within an increasingly-energetic srn stream jet \nforecast to strengthen from the subtropical ern N. Pacific (near and \neast of HI) to the GOM, and pivot across the South Atlantic states \nthis weekend. As it does so, it will likely interact with and \nenergize an initially east/west-oriented low-level baroclinic zone \nthat has become suppressed across far srn FL and The Bahamas, along \nthe srn periphery of an expansive and persistent anticyclone of cP \norigin across the ern US. The consensus of model guidance continues \nto favor cyclogenesis invof South FL and The Bahamas by Fri, with \nsubsequent increasing model spread in both strength and track of the \nlow off the South Atlantic coast through the weekend. Some of that \nmodel spread appears related to the aforementioned convective \nfeedback and forecast errors introduced, with the deterministic GFS \ndisplaying those symptoms most egregiously (and very likely \nerroneously) in recent days. Its consistent wwd/inland cyclone track \nthrough cntl NC, not coincidentally along the path of extreme QPF \nand development and amplification of low/mid-level vorticity maxima, \nis an extreme outlier and can be disregarded as a realistic \nsolution. \n\nGiven that signal of convective feedback even in the other dynamical \nguidance but generally to a lesser degree than in the GFS, the AI \nensemble guidance --which has not surprisingly displayed much better \nrun-to-run consistency than their physics-based deterministic and \nensemble counterparts--  is preferred. That guidance favors an \noffshore track generally parallel to the coast, but far enough west \nto spread meaningful rain through much of the ern half of NC during \nthe latter half of the weekend. Even small longitudinal adjustments \nto the favored cyclone track depicted by AI guidance would result in \nchanges in the location of the deformation, stratiform rain band, \nand its wrn edge, so forecast confidence in impacts/rain over cntl \nNC remains only low to medium. \n\nBeyond the probable weekend coastal low, the aforementioned \nenergetic srn stream will likely support at least one additional \nepisode of cyclogenesis across the wrn Atlantic Thanksgiving week, \nduring which time an early season Arctic high and airmass appears \npoised to spread from cntl Canada to the Great Lakes and nrn Middle \nAtlantic.",
